    {
      "content": "I work best when a conversation is messy, high-pressure, or full of hidden assumptions. My instinct is to turn uncertainty into something the group can measure, assign, and revisit. I care about plans that name failure modes clearly, give responsibility to real owners, and include checkpoints that can survive contact with operations. Much of this approach grew from my work at Eastwell Collaborative, where strategic goals had to be translated into practical implementation decisions.",
